#
# Tab-separated: name<TAB>file<TAB>from<TAB>to<TAB>suite<TAB>filter.  `\n` becomes a newline.
#
# The shared tail (`serve_named_channel`) is pinned by the `channel_by_name` suite as well, so
# a mutation inside it is caught twice over; the lines below aim at what is new — the team half,
# the two mux classes, and the validator order. The filter names both suites where a mutation
# is shared, because narrowing it to one would let the other decide the verdict.
#
# `validate_team_name_then_channel_name`'s parameter names run against the **unit** suite: the
# name reaches a client only through the i18n message, which this port does not serve ([D-092]),
# so the parity suite is blind to them.
#
# `api-channel-name-validator` survived the first run: the mux class and `IsValidChannelIdentifier`
# agree on almost everything, and a one-character channel name — the obvious probe — is *not*
# refused (there is no minimum-length rule, measured). The gap is the first character: the mux
# accepts a leading `-` or `_` and the validator does not. The suite now asks for both.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.
